// The indirect buffer's temporal stage (ENGINE-NOTES 7av).
//
// Four cosine rays a pixel cannot integrate a hemisphere. What makes them
// converge is accumulating a *different* four every frame, and until now the
// only thing doing that was whole-frame TAA -- which meant ray-traced GI was
// effectively TAA-only, grainy under every other anti-aliasing mode and under
// none. This pass accumulates the indirect term on its own, so convergence
// stops depending on which AA the profile happens to hold.
//
// **Both forms use this** (ENGINE-NOTES 7ay). It was the traced form's alone
// for one commit: the screen-space gather read the lit image, the lit image
// carried last frame's indirect, and accumulating its output compounded it to
// ten times the calibrated bleed -- a loop no constant in this shader could
// bound, because it ran one stage upstream. The gather now subtracts what the
// lit shader added, so its output no longer depends on its own history and
// this pass serves both.
//
// On a still camera it changes nothing for the screen-space form, and that is
// not a bug: its kernel is fixed and its estimate is the same every frame, so
// there is nothing to average. What it buys there is *motion* -- a reprojected
// history instead of a gather that pops with the screen content.
//
// The same shape as taa_resolve: reproject last frame through the velocity
// buffer 7.10 writes, reject what reprojected off screen, blend. Three
// differences, all because this buffer holds irradiance rather than a picture.
//
// **The feedback is much higher.** TAA settled on 0.6 because a picture that
// lags is worse than a picture that aliases. Indirect light is low frequency
// and has no edges to smear, so it can afford a long tail -- and it needs one,
// because the estimate underneath is four samples wide.
//
// **The neighbourhood clamp is weak here, and that is stated rather than
// hidden.** 7r's trap is that a clamp only rejects history where the
// neighbourhood *varies*, and indirect light is nearly uniform almost
// everywhere -- which is exactly where a wrong reprojection hides. So the clamp
// is kept (it costs one 3x3 read and catches the disocclusion cases that do
// vary) but it is not what makes this correct. The off-screen rejection is.
//
// **No YCoCg, no tonemapped clip.** Both exist in taa_resolve to protect a
// display-referred image from a single bright sample dragging the box. This
// buffer is linear irradiance with no highlights of its own; converting it
// would only cost precision at the low end, which is where a bounce lives.

#type vertex
#version 450 core

layout(location = 0) out vec2 v_UV;

void main()
{
	v_UV = vec2((gl_VertexIndex << 1) & 2, gl_VertexIndex & 2);
	gl_Position = vec4(v_UV * 2.0 - 1.0, 0.0, 1.0);
}

#type fragment
#version 450 core

layout(location = 0) in vec2 v_UV;
layout(location = 0) out vec4 o_Color;
// **What this pixel has learned about itself, for the next frame.**
//   .x  frames accumulated here, capped
//   .y  running mean of luminance
//   .z  running mean of luminance squared
// The colour target cannot carry these: its alpha is a validity flag the lit
// shader multiplies into the bounce, so it has to stay one. Written on every
// path, including the early outs -- an attachment a path forgets holds
// whatever was in the target, and the next frame would read it as a count.
layout(location = 1) out vec4 o_Moments;

// This frame's raw estimate: the screen-space gather's blurred output, or the
// lit shader's traced attachment. Point sampled -- the neighbourhood is read
// at exact texel offsets, and a filtered read would blur the box before the
// clip is computed from it.
layout(set = 0, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D u_Current;
// What this pass wrote last frame. Linear, because the reprojected position
// lands between texels whenever anything moves by a fraction of a pixel.
layout(set = 0, binding = 1) uniform sampler2D u_History;
// Screen-space motion, point sampled: a measurement per pixel, and the average
// of two pixels' motion is the motion of nothing.
layout(set = 0, binding = 2) uniform sampler2D u_Velocity;
// Last frame's counter and moments, reprojected with the colour. Point
// sampled: a count is not a thing to interpolate.
layout(set = 0, binding = 3) uniform sampler2D u_Moments;

layout(push_constant) uniform Params
{
	vec2  TexelSize;
	float Feedback;      // how much of the history survives, 0 disables
	float HasHistory;
	float HasMoments;    // whether u_Moments holds a real frame yet
	float FlipY;
} u_Params;

// How far outside the neighbourhood the history may sit before it is pulled
// back. Generous on purpose: see the note at the top about why a tight clamp
// on a near-uniform signal rejects almost nothing while costing the
// accumulation its tail.
const float kClampScale = 4.0;

// The same weighting the tone curve gives: what the eye reads as brightness.
float Luma(vec3 c)
{
	return dot(c, vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));
}

// **How many frames this pixel is allowed to remember.** The counter's only
// job is the warm-up -- once 1/n falls below the configured feedback the
// feedback takes over -- so it stops there rather than climbing forever and
// losing precision in a half float.
const float kMaxFrames = 16.0;

// **Accumulate in a range-compressed space, not in linear irradiance.**
//
// A mean is dragged by its largest term, and in linear light the largest term
// next to a bright fixture is enormous: one ray that finds the headlight's
// surround returns a value tens of times the frames around it, and every filter
// downstream is then averaging a number that is mostly that one sample. Bounds
// built from such a neighbourhood are useless -- beside a genuinely bright
// object the neighbours are bright too, so a spike only has to clear them by a
// little to read as a flash.
//
// Compressing by 1/(1+luma) makes a value's *weight* bounded while leaving its
// order intact, which is the same trick a weighted TAA resolve uses and for the
// same symptom. The whole estimate -- the firefly bound, the moments, the clamp
// and the blend -- happens in that space, and only the result comes back out.
// Doing it for some of them and not others would compare numbers in two
// different units.
vec3 Compress(vec3 c)
{
	return c / (1.0 + Luma(c));
}

vec3 Expand(vec3 c)
{
	// The exact inverse: luma(compressed) = L/(1+L), so 1 - that is 1/(1+L).
	return c / max(1.0 - Luma(c), 1e-4);
}

vec3 ClipToBox(vec3 history, vec3 centre, vec3 extent)
{
	vec3 offset = history - centre;
	vec3 units = abs(offset / max(extent, vec3(1e-5)));
	float furthest = max(units.x, max(units.y, units.z));
	return furthest > 1.0 ? centre + offset / furthest : history;
}

void main()
{
	const bool flip = u_Params.FlipY > 0.5;
	vec2 uv = vec2(v_UV.x, flip ? 1.0 - v_UV.y : v_UV.y);

	const vec4 current = texture(u_Current, uv);

	// Nothing to accumulate from: the chain's first frame, a resize, or the
	// feature switched off. Taking the current frame whole is what stops a
	// resumed history from being a lie -- the same contract taa_resolve has.
	if (u_Params.HasHistory < 0.5 || u_Params.Feedback <= 0.0)
	{
		// **Filtered even here**, which is the frame that needs it most: with
		// no history there is nothing but four rays, and this is the frame a
		// screenshot of a freshly loaded scene captures.
		vec3 nbSum = vec3(0.0);
		vec3 nbTop = vec3(0.0);
		for (int y = -1; y <= 1; y++)
		{
			for (int x = -1; x <= 1; x++)
			{
				if (x == 0 && y == 0)
					continue;
				const vec3 tap = texture(u_Current, uv + vec2(x, y) * u_Params.TexelSize).rgb;
				nbSum += tap;
				nbTop = max(nbTop, tap);
			}
		}

		// The same firefly bound the accumulating path applies, for the same
		// reason -- this is the frame with nothing to average against.
		const vec3 first = min(current.rgb, nbTop * 1.25 + vec3(0.05));
		const vec3 firstOut = max(mix(first, (nbSum + first) / 9.0, 0.65), vec3(0.0));
		o_Color = vec4(firstOut, 1.0);
		// One frame seen, and its luminance is the whole of what is known.
		const float firstLum = Luma(firstOut);
		o_Moments = vec4(1.0, firstLum, firstLum * firstLum, 0.0);
		return;
	}

	vec2 velocity = texture(u_Velocity, uv).xy;
	velocity.y = flip ? -velocity.y : velocity.y;
	const vec2 historyUV = uv - velocity;

	// Off the edge last frame means this surface was not on screen to be
	// estimated. Clamp-to-edge would hand back a neighbour's bounce, which is
	// how a wall picks up the glow of whatever was beside the frame.
	if (any(lessThan(historyUV, vec2(0.0))) || any(greaterThan(historyUV, vec2(1.0))))
	{
		o_Color = current;
		// Disoccluded: the count starts again here. Carrying the old one would
		// tell the next frame to trust an average of a surface that is not the
		// one now under this pixel.
		const float freshLum = Luma(current.rgb);
		o_Moments = vec4(1.0, freshLum, freshLum * freshLum, 0.0);
		return;
	}

	// Last frame's count and moments for this surface, fetched with the same
	// reprojection the colour uses.
	const vec4 prevMoments = u_Params.HasMoments > 0.5
						   ? texture(u_Moments, historyUV) : vec4(0.0);
	const float frames = min(prevMoments.x + 1.0, kMaxFrames);

	// The eight neighbours, kept apart from the centre: the box below needs
	// to know what the surroundings think *without* this pixel's own vote,
	// because this pixel's vote may be the firefly being judged.
	vec3 nbLowest = vec3(1e30);
	vec3 nbHighest = vec3(0.0);
	vec3 sum = vec3(0.0);
	// **The second moment, for the clamp below.** Free: it is the same nine
	// taps, squared. What it buys is a clamp width that knows the difference
	// between a neighbourhood that disagrees because the light genuinely
	// changes across it and one that disagrees because four rays are not many.
	vec3 sumSq = vec3(0.0);
	for (int y = -1; y <= 1; y++)
	{
		for (int x = -1; x <= 1; x++)
		{
			if (x == 0 && y == 0)
				continue;
			const vec3 tap = Compress(texture(u_Current,
									 uv + vec2(x, y) * u_Params.TexelSize).rgb);
			nbLowest = min(nbLowest, tap);
			nbHighest = max(nbHighest, tap);
			sum += tap;
			sumSq += tap * tap;
		}
	}

	// **The firefly clamp, and it is on the current frame, not the history.**
	//
	// The trace bounds each *sample* at 4.0, but the average of four can still
	// stand eighty times above every texel around it -- one ray grazing the
	// luminaire's bright surround does it. Unbounded, that spike entered the
	// blend at kSpatial's weight, spread into eight neighbours' means, blew
	// the clamp box open so the history clip stopped clipping, and then took
	// ten frames of feedback to fade: the white spots that show up near the
	// showroom's ceiling panel, measured swinging up to 90 display levels
	// frame to frame.
	//
	// A quarter above the brightest neighbour is the bound. Irradiance at
	// this resolution has no legitimate feature one texel wide -- the pass's
	// own argument for the spatial filter -- so anything further above its
	// whole neighbourhood than this is variance, not light. The number was
	// 2.0 first, and the showroom's ceiling kept flashing: beside the panel
	// the neighbours are legitimately bright, so a spike needs to clear them
	// by only a factor of two to read as an 85-level flash on screen. A real
	// change in lighting -- the showroom's switch -- raises the whole
	// neighbourhood together, so the bound rises with it and propagation is
	// untouched.
	const float kFireflyScale = 1.25;
	const vec3 bounded = min(Compress(current.rgb),
							 nbHighest * kFireflyScale + vec3(0.05));

	// The box, built from the *bounded* centre: a spike allowed to vote here
	// would widen the extent by exactly the amount it needed to survive the
	// history clip below.
	const vec3 lowest = min(nbLowest, bounded);
	const vec3 highest = max(nbHighest, bounded);
	const vec3 mean = (sum + bounded) / 9.0;

	// **The clamp is built from the neighbourhood's spread, not its extremes.**
	//
	// A min/max box is decided by its two most extreme taps, which on a
	// four-ray estimate are the two least trustworthy numbers in it: one stray
	// sample opens the box by however much it strays, and a box opened by noise
	// stops clipping the history it exists to clip. The mean and the standard
	// deviation are decided by all nine, so a neighbourhood that is genuinely
	// uniform produces a tight box however unlucky one tap was, and one that
	// straddles a real lighting edge produces a wide one -- which is the same
	// self-adaptation SVGF gets from its variance estimate, without the spatial
	// filter that estimate normally drives.
	//
	// **Never looser than the box it replaces.** The old width is kept as a
	// ceiling, so this can only tighten and never widen: where the estimate is
	// noisy enough that sigma is large, behaviour is what it was, and the gain
	// is confined to where the neighbourhood agrees.
	//
	// kSigma is the one dial. Higher trusts the history further and ghosts;
	// lower clips more and lets noise through. Two standard deviations covers
	// ~95% of a normal neighbourhood.
	const float kSigma = 2.0;
	const vec3 meanSq = (sumSq + bounded * bounded) / 9.0;
	const vec3 sigma = sqrt(max(meanSq - mean * mean, vec3(0.0)));

	const vec3 boxCentre = (lowest + highest) * 0.5;
	const vec3 boxExtent = max(highest - boxCentre, vec3(1e-4)) * kClampScale;

	// **The pixel's own history of itself, which the neighbours cannot supply.**
	// Spatial spread says how much this *area* disagrees; temporal spread says
	// how much this *pixel* has been fluctuating. They answer different
	// questions, and the second is the one a temporal filter needs.
	const float lum = Luma(bounded);
	// The warm-up. With few frames behind it the estimate should move freely;
	// once 1/n falls below the configured feedback, the feedback governs and
	// this stops mattering. Without it a fresh pixel crawls toward the answer
	// at the feedback rate, which at 0.9 is thirty frames of visibly wrong.
	const float alpha = max(1.0 / frames, 1.0 - u_Params.Feedback);
	const float momMean = mix(prevMoments.y, lum, alpha);
	const float momMeanSq = mix(prevMoments.z, lum * lum, alpha);
	const float temporalSigma = sqrt(max(momMeanSq - momMean * momMean, 0.0));

	o_Moments = vec4(frames, momMean, momMeanSq, 0.0);

	const vec3 centre = mean;
	// **Never clamp tighter than this pixel is known to fluctuate.** A box
	// narrower than the pixel's own temporal noise rejects history every frame
	// on a pixel that was never wrong, and a history rejected every frame is
	// four rays with no accumulation at all -- the noise the pass exists to
	// remove. The spatial estimate still provides the ceiling.
	const vec3 floored = max(min(sigma * kSigma, boxExtent),
							 vec3(temporalSigma * kSigma));
	const vec3 extent = max(floored, vec3(1e-4));

	// **The spatial half, which this pass did not have.**
	//
	// It accumulated across time and never filtered across space: the 3x3
	// neighbourhood above was read only to *clamp* the history and then thrown
	// away. That leaves the estimate as noisy as four rays make it on the
	// frames after a disocclusion, and permanently noisy anywhere the history
	// keeps being rejected -- which is exactly where the eye is looking, because
	// disocclusion follows motion.
	//
	// Indirect light is the lowest-frequency thing in the frame and this buffer
	// is already at half resolution, so nine taps two pixels wide cost almost
	// nothing in accuracy and divide the variance by up to nine. The taps are
	// the ones the clamp already fetched, so it is free.
	//
	// **Blended rather than replaced**, and the weight is not one: a box filter
	// over a disocclusion boundary pulls in a neighbour that is looking at
	// different geometry, and at full strength that is a halo. The clamp above
	// bounds how far the *history* may stray; this bounds how far the current
	// estimate may.
	const float kSpatial = 0.65;
	const vec3 filtered = mix(bounded, mean, kSpatial);

	vec4 history = texture(u_History, historyUV);
	history.rgb = ClipToBox(Compress(history.rgb), centre, extent);

	// `alpha` is how much of this frame to take, so 1 - alpha is how much of
	// the history survives -- the configured feedback once the pixel is warm,
	// and less than it while it is not.
	vec3 result = mix(filtered, history.rgb, 1.0 - alpha);

	// Negative irradiance is not a thing; a filtered read across a disocclusion
	// can produce one, and it would darken the surface below unlit.
	//
	// **Alpha is written as one, not carried from the input** (ENGINE-NOTES
	// 7ay). The lit shader multiplies this buffer's alpha into the bounce, so
	// it is a validity flag and nothing else -- and the screen-space chain
	// arrives here with *linear depth* in alpha, which its blur uses for its
	// edge test. Passing that through multiplied the bounce by a distance in
	// metres: measured +6.93 against a correct +1.27, and it looked exactly
	// like a feedback loop rather than like a unit error.
	// Back to linear irradiance, which is what the lit shader multiplies in.
	o_Color = vec4(max(Expand(result), vec3(0.0)), 1.0);
}
